      "text": "Lake Guatavita and Zipaquirá"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:4b5ff9b4a37f43cc",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "ed1492cc2e1cdf3f7d072b5638be429e3301d37d8764d9ba17e87588de98b3aa",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261109-mw9pzmb9-dxwx6p3k",
        "model": "GPT-6.1 Sol"
      },
      "text": "After breakfast, meet your English-speaking guide and set out from Bogotá for a full-day trip.\n\nYour first stop is Lake Guatavita, 57 km from the capital at an elevation of 3,100 m. The lake is associated with the legend of El Dorado. During the investiture of a new cacique, the chief would go out onto the lake on a raft while participants in the ceremony cast gold figurines known as tunjos into the water. The story repeatedly drew treasure hunters to Guatavita. Their attempts to find gold on the lake bed became part of its history, and the legend connects this natural landmark to the region’s pre-Columbian culture.\n\nAfter visiting the lake, continue to Zipaquirá, a drive of about an hour and a half. There you visit the Catedral de Sal, a cathedral carved entirely out of salt and situated 180 m below sea level. After the tour, there’s time for a stroll and to shop for souvenirs. Have lunch at a restaurant, then return to Bogotá to rest."
    },
